\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Norway Currency Interventions \highlight\uc2  Unlikely For Now -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2020\par 12:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1048 GMT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Norway\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to shelve its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as the krone recovers against the euro, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. "Unless there is a renewed wave of NOK selling, there is no need for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  with EUR/NOK slowly drifting further downwards," Commerzbank \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Antje Praefcke\uc2  says. Oil prices and risk appetite will continue to drive the krone's moves in the short term, she says. EUR/NOK falls 0.4% to 10.9901.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1038 GMT - CreditSights is advising clients not to buy \uc2 Telefonica\uc2  's 7.25-year and 12-year euro bonds on offer Wednesday at a spread lower than 32 basis points below their initial price talks. This means buying the 7.25-year bond at a spread up to 133 bps over the mid-swap level, which implies a yield of 1.08% and buying the 12-year bond at a spread of up to 173 bps over mid-swaps, implying a yield of 1.65%. These levels offer a new premium concession of 5 basis points over \uc2 Telefonica\uc2  's comparable existing bonds, says the credit research firm. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 1023 GMT - The 11.3% decline in the eurozone's industrial production in March is massive, but it will be even worse for April, says \uc2 Bert Colijn\uc2  , eurozone senior economist at ING. "From there on, there will be significant recovery as the reopening of plants will surely increase production from the very low April bottom," Colijn says. The recovery will be gradual due to the slow easing of measures, supply chain disruptions amid an uneven reopening of the global economy, and demand at lower levels than before the coronavirus crisis, he says. "It seems likely that production levels of 2017 will be out of sight for a long time to come," Colijn says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1003 GMT - The \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  probably won't take interest rates into negative territory but speculation of such a move could build and send the dollar lower, \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  says. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  's call for negative rates and expectations for a rate cut in the futures markets mean a speech by Fed Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  later Wednesday "will be important," \uc2 MUFG\uc2 's \uc2 Derek Halpenny\uc2  says. "Anything less than a forthright rejection of negative rates could well fuel further speculation of negative rates being possible," he says. "A deeper negative yield implied in the futures market could well start to weigh more on the dollar and is a source of downside risk we previously had not considered." The dollar index is steady at 99.954. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0958 GMT - The effects of the corona epidemic that \uc2 Nordea\uc2  had expected in Sweden's March inflation numbers came in April instead. Inflation is currently a bit tricky to interpret, but there is little doubt that price pressures are very low, \uc2 Nordea\uc2  says. "We expect the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  to leave the repo rate unchanged at zero, but the record low inflation highlight risks of a rate cut." (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0956 GMT - While it still remains unlikely, negative U.S. interest rates would give gold a "massive boost," Carsten Fritsch at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. Negative real rates have been one of the main drivers of gold's recent strength and expectations that they will linger for some time have led many analysts to expect gold to rise further this year. Negative interest rates would further push down real rates and reduce the opportunity cost of holding gold. "So far, the Fed has ruled out any such step" to cut rates below zero, says Fritsch, and it would be very surprising if Powell were to express a different tone" when he speaks later today. Still, speculation continues with some fed fund futures pricing in the possibility, he notes. (william.horner@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0921 GMT - Almost one-third of people in the UK expect to face a pay cut or lose their job in the next two to four weeks, according to a survey conducted by \uc2 UBS\uc2  . The poll results, based on 1,000 responses, also shows that about half of them have no savings or have enough to last for less than three months. 42% of the respondents expect their financial situation to worsen in the next year, the medium-income group being the most pessimistic. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT - While the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  will look through the energy-driven plunge in Swedish headline inflation , the further fall in underlying price pressures will be harder to ignore, so it's increasingly a question of "when" rather than "if" the bank cuts again, says \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  . With resource utilisation falling off a cliff, underlying inflation is set for a prolonged period below the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  's 2.0% target, it says. While minutes published earlier this week showed the governing council did not think a policy rate cut would be effective at the moment, they appear to be keeping their powder dry until the recovery phase gathers pace, Capital Eocnomics says. "Accordingly, we still expect the bank to cut its policy rate back into negative territory later in the year."(dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0910 GMT - The pound's modest gains in reaction to better-than-forecast first-quarter U.K. economic growth data are likely to be brief, ActivTrades says. Sterling's respite is "unlikely to be long-lived" as March, when the lockdown started, registered a record 5.8% monthly decline in gross domestic product, suggesting the second quarter figures will be "considerably worse," ActivTrades analyst \uc2 Ricardo Evangelista\uc2  says. The U.K. economy contracted 2.0% in the first quarter, according to the official preliminary estimate Wednesday, although that was smaller than the consensus forecast of 2.5%, he says. GBP/USD rises 0.1% to 1.2270 and EUR/GBP falls 0.2% to 0.8834. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0909 GMT - The Japanese yen should continue to strengthen against the dollar as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  declines on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  's actions, Societe Generale \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Kit Juckes says. "The yen continues to disappoint those who think it should do better in times of crisis, yet it is still out-performing the dollar even as the latter benefits from its dominant status," Juckes says. "That makes long yen attractive for anyone who think that it is only a matter of time before the U.S. makes a more concerted effort to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." The Fed could face pressure from President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to cut interest rates further to curb the dollar's strength, he says. USD/JPY falls 0.1% to 107.012. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0907 GMT - The U.K.'s first-quarter economic contraction of 2.0% compares favorably with many other countries but that doesn't mean it is better placed than others, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  says. "Recall that the U.K. did not enter full lockdown until March 23," \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist \uc2 Elizabeth Martins\uc2  says. "Although clearly, activity will have started to tail off before then, this 2% fall for an entire quarter actually reflects only a single week of full lockdown." The numbers for the second quarter "will be much worse," Martins says. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  expects the economy to shrink by a quarterly 13% in the second quarter as the U.K. remains largely locked down six weeks into the period while other European economies start to reopen. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0849 GMT - A marked rise in U.K. unemployment is likely to limit the economy's recovery after the coronavirus lockdown eases, EY Item Club says after data showed gross domestic product fell 2.0% in the first quarter. "EY Item Club predicts that the economy is unlikely to return to its size of the fourth quarter of 2019 until the first quarter of 2023," EY economist \uc2 Howard Archer\uc2  says. The U.K. economic forecasting group now expects U.K. GDP to drop 7.5% in 2020, compared to its previous estimate for a 6.8% decline. It also downgrades its forecast for second quarter GDP to a 15% quarterly fall from a 13% decrease. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 13, 2020 06:48 ET (10:48 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200513eg5d001rz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
