\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Imf\uc2 ? EUR/Usd skidded to 2017 lows, \uc2 ECB\uc2  announces purchases of ?750 mM\b0\par\par\uc2 967 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Eur/Nok renews historical highs; \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Norway\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market; Eur/Usd skidded to 2017 lows, \uc2 ECB\uc2  announces purchases of \u8364\'20\'AC750mM; Oil sinks again, crude hit 18-year lows; Gold continues to retreat, but appears to stabilize Eur/Nok renews historical highs; \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Norway\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the marketAs had happened last week, the \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  has cut interest rates again in an attempt to alleviate the economic impact of Covid-19. This time the cut was 75 bp and took the director rate from 1% to 0.25% - new historical low. This cut followed the 50 bp made in the previous week, when the Governor of the Bank indicated that the economy of the Nordic country is in a state of emergency. The committee's decision was unanimous, revealing that after last week's cut, "the situation in the Norwegian economy continued to worsen." The Committee also revealed that it was available to make further cuts.\par \par \uc2  For the Norwegian krone, March has been quite difficult, having fallen more than 26% against the euro to new historic lows, above 13 noks per euro. Contributing to the devaluation of the crown is the sharp fall in oil prices (see text below). The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  made an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, which allowed a recovery of the crown.Technically, Eur/Nok intensified the uptrend after breaking to the upper limit of the ascending channel (dashed red), renewing new historical highs above 13 noks. The indicators show some saturation of the bullish movement.EUR/Usd skidded to 2017 lows, \uc2 ECB\uc2  announces purchases of \u8364\'20\'AC750mMMIn the last week, all the attention turned (unsurprisingly) to the Covid-19 and its impact on world economies. We believe it is not unreasonable to consider that the markets have entered, if they had not already done so, in panic. So much has happened that it is even difficult to follow all developments. Nevertheless, the \uc2 ECB\uc2 's huge \u8364\'20\'AC750m bond-buying programme is highlighted to prevent the start of a new debt crisis. This program came with the promise of removing self-imposed "restrictions" if necessary - a reference to the limit of owning more than a third of a country's obligation. This means that purchases can be made on the bonds that are most needed, which means that the Bank can increase purchases of bonds from Italy, for example. The U.S. has also announced an incentive package and says it is preparing another. Additionally, the EDF revealed that it has established a temporary program to supply nine more Central Banks to alleviate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shortages. Swaps, in which the EDF has established agreements with other central banks to accept \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in exchange for dollars, will allow at least the central banks of Australia, Brazil, South Korea, Mexico, Singapore, Sweden, Denmark, for at least the next six months, Norway and New Zealand get a combined total of $450 mM to ensure the proper functioning of the financial system.Technically, Eur/Usd presents a still bearish perspective, having broken down the lower limit of the downward trend channel (red-dashed). The pair appear to have some stability around $1.07. However, macdcontinues to show a strong sell signal, indicating that if the pair fails to highlight in this line, it may follow up the losses against $1.05 in the short term.Oil re-sank, crude at 18-year lowsOil prices have lived through another very troubled week. The raw material again recorded sharp falls, to 2002 lows in the case of crude and 2003 in the case of Brent. Putting pressure on prices is the great imbalance between supply and demand. This has been exacerbated by the price war between Saudi Arabia and Russia, at a time when prospects for demand are still being revised downwards due to the impact of the Covid-19 pandemic. Given the time duration of the latest price wars, the losses to oil may not be over yet. Despite the falls, it should be noted that at the end of the week prices partially recovered after various stimulus from the largest global economies and after indications that Donald Trump could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the price war between Saudi Arabia and Russia.Technically, crude continued to fall, eventually breaking the 100% fibonacci retraction, reaching 2017 lows. Apart from this, there is not much news to report on the technical analysis in black gold. The outlook is clearly pointed downwards, with MACD and RSI supporting this trend. The next significance support is at the psychological level of $20/barrel.Gold continues to retreat, but appears to have stabilizedDespite the significant increase in risk aversion, due to fears surrounding Covid-19, gold prices have again recorded a negative weekly balance. Justifying the drop will again be the need for investors to sell the metal to meet liquidity needs to address the extreme volatility that shakes global markets. Despite this, at the end of the week, losses were eventually limited.Technically, gold followed the falls, reaching December lows. Nevertheless, despite the macd's sell-off signal, the precious metal appears to have found support around $1450, a level at which it stood out, and is currently testing the 61.8% fibonacci retraction (around $1500/ounce). A break at this level would pave the way for $1550.The technical analyses published herein do not intend, under any circumstances, to constitute advice or a recommendation for the purchase and sale of financial instruments, so analysts and the Business Journal cannot be liable for any losses or damages that result from the use of this information. If you would like to see any questions clearabout the Technical Analysis, please contact the \uc2 IMF\uc2  or the Business Journal.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020200323eg3n0023a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
