\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED-WRAPUP 1-Dollar rampage spurs  \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions \highlight\uc2 , speculation of big G7 move\b0\par\par\uc2 918 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par 18:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Corrects March 19 version of the story to fix dollar gain in paragraph 4)\par \par \uc2 By Karin Strohecker and Sujata Rao\par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 19 (Reuters) - From Brazil to Norway, policymakers are leaping to defend \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the onslaught of the dollar which scaled three-year peaks on Thursday, raising speculation that a joint move by the world's biggest central banks may be in the offing.\par \par \uc2 Unlikely, most market-watchers say. But these are unusual times, and \b \highlight22\uc2 Norway\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the day threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lift the crown, a step it hasn't taken in more than 20 years.\par \par \uc2 The warning followed the crown's 30% plunge versus the dollar in less than three weeks, though oil's price collapse contributed.\par \par \uc2 But the dollar's brutal ascent -- up 5% this month against a basket of peers -- has sent almost every other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reeling. Amid the crisis caused by worries about how badly the coronavirus pandemic will hit the world economy, investors and companies around the world have been scrambling to get their hands on the market's most liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That forced highly unexpected moves such as a 15 basis-point rate rise from Denmark to support its crown while other countries such as Russia and Brazil engaged in direct dollar-buying. And South Africa said it did not rule out emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Despite these efforts, a global stampede for dollar funding meant \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the world sank to multi-year or record lows against the greenback. Some relief came after the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  opened $450 billion in swap lines to several central banks but the pressure broadly remains.\par \par \uc2 "If there is one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  causing problems right now and aggravating the sell-off in global asset markets, it is the U.S. dollar," \uc2 ING Bank\uc2  told clients.\par \par \uc2 Could the answer lie in large-scale global \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame the greenback?\par \par \uc2 Most top central banks, with the exception perhaps of Switzerland, are committed to letting markets determine exchange rates. Yet many welcomed Norway's shift; Danske Bank economist Frank Jullum urged it to buy crowns "by the bucketful".\par \par \uc2 One issue is that piecemeal \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are often doomed to fail as individual economies usually lack the firepower to influence \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for any length of time But shock-and-awe moves such as the coordinated \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  moves to weaken the yen, seen after the 9/11 terror attacks or the 2011 Japan tsunami, have worked.\par \par \uc2 Alternatively the United States could act alone, some say, noting that dollar strength was highly unwelcome at a time when the U.S. economy is headed for recession. The last time the Fed went out on its own was in the early 1990s under George H.W. Bush to stem a soaring dollar.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. has brought in almost all its instruments, the one that hasn't been done is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the stronger the dollar gets the more likely the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  will consider \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Thomas Flury, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategies at UBS Global Wealth Management's Chief Investment Office.\par \par \uc2 "Typical during past crises, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped calm not only \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets but also equities and brought credit spreads down and that is something they will be keen to do." \par \par \uc2 \f2               \par               \par               \par               \par OUTSIZE DOLLAR\par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar strength will dismay not only those in the Trump administration, but others around the world because when the dollar rises, it tends to 'tighten' conditions through channels such as higher import prices or by forcing interest rate hikes to shield \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That in turn risks triggering a full-blown emerging markets crisis, the pattern seen many times in the 20th century.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Its power is down to the outsize role it plays in keeping the world's financial systems ticking over, accounting for 40% of global trade flows, 50% of cross-border lending and 65-85% of the world's risk-free assets, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Dollar strength is unwelcome for the U.S. as it tightens financial conditions but for the rest of world it tightens financial conditions even more because many countries are highly dollarised or they have dollar debt," said Claire Dissaux, head of global economics & strategy at Millennium Global Investments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 She doubts so far the pain from the dollar is big enough for Washington to ponder \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  -- alone or with G7 peers -- and says containing the raging selloff in equity markets will be the greater focus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Plus there are the logistics of coordinating such a move with other central banks, many of which will not be keen to see their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strengthening even as world growth nosedives.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Without "credible commitment from a group of central banks it won't work," said Dissaux who believes the best option is more swap lines of the sort the Fed announced on Thursday\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But it is not just the dollar rise that is hurting, but also the brutal volatility. Implied volatility, or expected price swings for some of the world's biggest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have rocketed to multi-year highs -- one-month Norwegian crown vol is over 30%, triple end-February levels. The euro, pound and all emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  paint a similar picture.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The vols are unbelievable. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would quickly kick that lower," Flury of UBS Wealth said, calling it "the fear factor".\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Karin Strohecker and Sujata Rao, additional reporting by Tommy Wilkes; Editing by Chizu Nomiyama) \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-3-20T18:03:26.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200319eg3j04zjx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
