\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4- \highlight22\uc2 Norway \highlight\uc2  crown rallies as  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  says it may  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par 13:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Crown has lost one third of its value this year\par \par \uc2 * Fell in wake of coronavirus, oil price crash\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Norway\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Jan 1999\par \par \uc2 * Also pledges more cash for banks\par \par \uc2 * 'Buy by the bucketful', Danske Bank's chief economist says (Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move, liquidity for banks)\par \par \uc2 By Terje Solsvik and Gwladys Fouche\par \par \uc2 OSLO, March 19 (Reuters) - \uc2 Norway's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday it is considering entering the market to buy its own crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , triggering a rebound in the crown, which had lost a third of its value this year, in a step it has not taken since early 1999.\par \par \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  will also pump more cash into the banking system, offering loans lasting for up to 12 months to help alleviate effects of the coronavirus pandemic and a slide in the price of crude oil, Norway's biggest export.\par \par \uc2 The crown rallied following the statements, erasing intra-day losses against the euro, although \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  declined to say whether any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had taken place.\par \par \uc2 Danske Bank Chief Economist Frank Jullum urged \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  to buy "by the bucketful".\par \par \uc2 The crown rose to 12.2870 against the euro at 1144 GMT from an all-time low of 13.1678 earlier in the day, Refinitiv data showed.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  is continuously considering whether there is a need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market by purchasing Norwegian kroner," \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 's Jullum said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should buy heavily and refrain from announcing the volume of its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "One advice: please don't say how much per day, per week, etc or how much altogether, or what is the target for the NOK. Just go into the market and buy crowns, quietly, and by the bucketful," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the crown in January 1999, when its official goal was to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a narrow band against those of its trading partners.\par \par \uc2 In 2001, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's mandate switched to an inflation target, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has since traded freely, even during the volatile months of the 2008-2009 financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 "In 2009 the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen the NOK was not a credible strategy over time. But they can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they see big, short-term movements, for instance due to reduced liquidity in the market," tweeted Handelsbanken Chief Economist Kari Due-Andresen.\par \par \uc2 Norway has huge foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, including the world's largest sovereign fund - worth $880 billion as of Thursday - although its value has tumbled from $1.17 trillion on Feb. 13 as global stock markets plunged. (Reporting by Terje Solsvik and Gwladys Fouche; Editing by Alex 