\par\par \b \uc2 Nordic Morning Briefing: Swedish Elections in Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jens Hansegard   \par \uc2 2,847 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2014\par 04:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR - All times GMT \par Nordic Macro \par  \par 0800 Norway Aug Trade \par  \par Nordic Equities \par  \par 0600 \f28 \uc2 \f2 H&M\f28 \uc2 \f2  3Q and Aug Sales \par  \par  \par IN FOCUS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The focus Monday will be on the results of Sweden's general election.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Sweden's Social Democrat Leader Stefan Lofven defeated incumbent Prime Minister \uc2 Fredrik Reinfeldt\uc2  in parliamentary elections on Sunday, signaling the return of a left-leaning government after eight years in opposition.\par \par \uc2 The shift reflected concerns among the Swedish electorate that Mr. Reinfeldt's pro-market policies have chipped away at the country's cherished welfare state. Mr. Reinfeldt said he would resign as prime minister on Monday and as leader of his party by spring.\par \par \uc2 With nearly all votes counted, results from Sweden's election authority showed the Social Democrats won 31.1% of the vote, largely unchanged from the previous election in 2010, while Mr. Reinfeldt's Moderate Party slumped to 23.2%, from 30.1% at the previous election.\par \par \uc2 The Swedish elections don't have a history of moving markets but concerns have arisen over what will happen if the parties cannot agree on forming a government. Mr. Lofven failed to secure an absolute majority and faces tough negotiations with left-leaning allies over the formation of a government.\par \par \uc2 On the corporate agenda, Swedish clothing retailer \uc2 H&M\uc2  will publish third-quarter sales. Full earnings will be disclosed on Sept. 25.\par \par \uc2 Later in the week, attention will turn to Norway where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to leave its main interest rate unchanged. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par  \par EUR/NOK \par Latest 0150 GMT 8.2474-562 \par Previous 2150 GMT 8.2460-565 \par %Chg +0.01 \par  \par EUR/SEK \par Latest 0150 GMT 9.2613-90 \par Previous 2150 GMT 9.2626-764 \par %Chg -0.05 \par  \par USD/NOK \par Latest 0150 GMT 6.3617-76 \par Previous 2150 GMT 6.3573-635 \par %Chg +0.07 \par  \par USD/SEK \par Latest 0150 GMT 7.1439-90 \par Previous 2150 GMT 7.1409-91 \par %Chg +0.02 \par  \par  \par NORDIC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monday, all eyes will be on the results of the Swedish parliamentary election.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With nearly all votes counted from Sunday's election, the results from the election authority show the Social Democrats won 31.1% of the vote, largely unchanged from the previous election in 2010, while support for the Moderate Party slumped to 23.2%, from 30.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swedish elections don't have a history of moving markets but concerns over what will happen if the parties cannot agree on forming a government have arisen recently. Mr. Lofven will face tough negotiations with left-leaning allies over forming a coalition government after failing to secure an absolute majority.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nordea\f28 \uc2 \f2  has said a change of government could bring tax hikes affecting the inflation path from January 2015.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Later in the week, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norway's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to leave its main interest rate unchanged as inflation remains close to the 2.5% target.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All analysts in a Wall Street Journal poll said they expect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  to leave its benchmark rate at 1.5%. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCK INDEXES: \par  \par OMXN40  1356.00  +5.36  +0.4% \par OMXS30  1388.59  +5.74  +0.4% \par OBX      561.93  +2.72  +0.5% \par OMXC20   759.04  +7.82  +1.0% \par OMXH25  2939.65  -6.29  -0.2% \par  \par Brent    $96.48 -$0.63  -0.7% \par  \par NORDIC MARKETS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nordic stocks edged higher Friday as investors continued to weigh the prospects of a change of government in Sweden in the general election. In corporate news, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Statoil\f28 \uc2 \f2  ended 0.5% higher after announcing it had sold several oil and gas assets to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wintershall AG\f28 \uc2 \f2  for up to $1.3 billion, and will use the cash to invest heavily in new projects.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the corporate agenda, \f28 \uc2 \f2 H&M\f28 \uc2 \f2  publishes 3Q sales figures at 0600 GMT. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures continued lower in Asian trade, extending last week's falls which saw \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  crude end the week 1% lower and Brent down 3.7%, its largest weekly decline since early July.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Supply is overwhelming the market, and demand's not really keeping up with it," said Oliver Sloup, director of managed futures at brokerage iiTrader in Chicago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Prices in both markets are expected to remain weak as northern hemisphere refineries schedule maintenance in September/October, keeping crude demand low," ANZ said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brent crude was 63 cents lower at $96.48/bbl, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  was $1.03 lower at $91.24/bbl. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEWS ROUNDUP: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: Sweden's Social Democrat Leader Stefan Lofven defeated incumbent Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fredrik Reinfeldt\f28 \uc2 \f2  in parliamentary elections on Sunday, signaling the return of a left-leaning government after eight years in opposition.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NORWAY: Norway's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Statoil ASA\f28 \uc2 \f2  said on Friday that it had sold several oil and gas assets to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wintershall AG\f28 \uc2 \f2  for up to $1.3 billion, and said that it would use the cash to invest heavily in new projects.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: Swedish construction company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Skanska AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  (SKA-B.SK) said Friday it has signed a $150 million contract to renovate and expand \f28 \uc2 \f2 Riverside Community Hospital\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Riverside, California, U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINLAND: Finnish airline \f28 \uc2 \f2 Finnair\f28 \uc2 \f2  (FIA1S.HE) said Friday it has appointed \f28 \uc2 \f2 Juha Jarvinen\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief commercial officer and a member of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Finnair\f28 \uc2 \f2 's executive board as of November 1, 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: Swedish online search and catalog company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eniro AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ENRO.SK) said Friday that its auditor, PwC, has presented the second part of its investigation into the company's accounting and it gives the company no reason to change its previously issued full year guidance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: Danish jewelry retailer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pandora A/S\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PNDORA.KO) has signed an agreement with distributor Bluebell over the joint distribution of Pandora jewellery in Japan from 1 January 2015, it said Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Write to Jens Hansegard at jens.hansegard@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 15 Sep 2014 22:50 ET \par Nordic Morning Briefing: Eyes on New Swedish Government\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Jens Hansegard \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR - All times GMT \par  \par Other Macro \par  \par 0830  U.K \f28 \uc2 \f2 CPI\f28 \uc2 \f2  Aug. \par 0900  Germany ZEW Index Sept. \par 1230  U.S. PPI Aug. \par  \par  \par IN FOCUS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tuesday, the focus in Sweden will remain on the new government. Sunday's general election left Prime Minister elect Stefan Lofven with work to do to form a stable and durable coalition after his center left party and its allies won most votes but fell short of a majority.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Lofven now faces tough and urgent negotiations with left-leaning allies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The corporate agenda is thin on Tuesday, but Germany's Zew index tends to set the tone for the Nordic stock market. Last month's ZEW index hit a 20-month low, and now forecasts are for an even bigger drop, amid deteriorating growth, mounting deflationary pressure and the squeeze caused by sanctions and conflict with Russia over its incursions into Ukraine.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Later in the week, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  investors may take a look at the Riksbank minutes, due to be published on Wednesday. On Thursday attention will turn to Norway where the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to leave its main interest rate unchanged. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par  \par EUR/NOK \par Latest 0150 GMT 8.2710-96 \par Previous 2150 GMT 8.2692-767 \par %Chg +0.03 \par  \par EUR/SEK \par Latest 0150 GMT 9.2019-70 \par Previous 2150 GMT 9.2039-112 \par %Chg -0.03 \par  \par USD/NOK \par Latest 0150 GMT 6.3835-900 \par Previous 2150 GMT 6.3909-56 \par %Chg -0.10 \par  \par USD/SEK \par Latest 0150 GMT 7.1019-56 \par Previous 2150 GMT 7.1132-77 \par %Chg -0.16 \par  \par NORDIC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The political uncertainty created by Sunday's Swedish election result is weighing on the krona, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nordea\f28 \uc2 \f2  said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Social Democrats are in the driving seat to form a new government, taking over from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fredrik Reinfeldt\f28 \uc2 \f2 's center right alliance, but the result was indecisive, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Torbjorn Isaksson\f28 \uc2 \f2  at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nordea\f28 \uc2 \f2  bank said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It may take several weeks or even longer to form a new government and several alternatives are possible," he said. Much suggests that economic policy will be characterized by compromises over the coming years and that it will be difficult for the new left-leaning government to implement its proposed reforms, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, the election doesn't change much for the Riksbank in the near term. Inflation is in focus for the bank and that will only be affected to a minor extent by the election.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swedish krona looks set to weaken slightly over the next few months, SEB bank said. This is mainly because the Riksbank is likely to follow the ECB and carry out further monetary stimulus in the autumn, but the uncertain political situation will also weigh, SEB said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Monday, Sweden's finance minister of eight years, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Anders Borg\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said he was leaving party politics.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Borg has been a high-profile public figure alongside Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fredrik Reinfeldt\f28 \uc2 \f2  over two terms in government and had been seen as a possible candidate to replace Mr. Reinfeldt as leader of the center-right Moderate Party. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCK INDEXES: \par  \par OMXN40  1361.11  +5.11  +0.4% \par OMXS30  1393.22  +4.63  +0.3% \par OBX      560.06  -1.87  -0.3% \par OMXC20   759.05  +0.01  flat \par OMXH25  2937.49  -2.16  -0.1% \par  \par Brent    $98.07 +$0.19  +0.2% \par  \par NORDIC MARKETS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nordic stocks ended mixed Monday, as investors weighed the implications of a change of government in Sweden after Sunday's general election.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In corporate news, TDC ended 10.5% lower after saying it will buy Norwegian cable TV company Get AS for $2.2 billion as it broadens its Scandinavian reach into Norway. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures were mixed in Asian trade after a slide Monday that saw Brent crude close at the lowest settlement since June 28, 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The supply overhang in major \f28 \uc2 \f2 OECD\f28 \uc2 \f2  markets is likely to continue to weigh on prices across the next month or so, before more price-supportive winter demand conditions toward the end of the year," ANZ said. Dollar strength also remains a headwind, it added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brent crude was 19 cents higher at $98.07/bbl, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  was 7 cents lower at $92.85/bbl. The October Brent contract expired at the close of trading Monday. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEWS ROUNDUP: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINLAND: Finland's Prime Minister Alexander Stubb said he is confident his government will remain in office despite a junior party's threat to quit the government over a proposal to allow a Finnish-Russian consortium to proceed with its controversial nuclear power-plant project in Finland.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: Swedish retailer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hennes & Mauritz\f28 \uc2 \f2  (HM.B-SK) said Monday its August sales increased by 19% compared with the same period last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN's Minister of Defense Sten Tolgfors resigned following a three-week long controversy over Swedish arms affairs with Saudi Arabia. Earlier this month, Swedish Radio reported that the defense research agency FOI had plans to assist Saudi Arabia in building an advanced weapons-making factory.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NORWAY: Norway's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aker Solutions ASA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (AKSO.OS) said Monday its Aker Oilfield Services (AKOFS) unit has been awarded a five-year $465 million contract from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petrobras\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PBR) to provide subsea \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  services offshore Brazil.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 NORWAY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : Norwegian seafood company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Marine Harvest ASA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (MHG) said Monday it has agreed to buy the assets of former Chilean farming company Acuinova Chile S.A., currently in bankruptcy, for $120 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NORWAY: Danish telecommunications company \f28 \uc2 \f2 TDC A/S\f28 \uc2 \f2  ( TDC.KO) will buy Norwegian cable TV company Get AS for 13.8 billion Norwegian kroner ($2.2 billion) as it broadens its Scandinavian reach into Norway.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Write to Jens Hansegard at jens.hansegard@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16 Sep 2014 22:24 ET \par Nordic Morning Briefing: Central Banks in Focus\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Charles Duxbury \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR - All times GMT \par Nordic Macro \par 0730 Sweden Riksbank Minutes \par 0900 Sweden Bond Auction \par  \par Other Macro \par 0830 Bank Of England Minutes \par 0900 EU HICP Final \par 1100 U.S. Mortgage \par Applications \par 1200 Russia Retail Sales \par 1230 U.S. CPI \par 1230 U.S. Current Account \par 1800 U.S. FOMC Rate Call \par 1800 U.S. Fed MBS/Treasury \par Purchases \par  \par  \par IN FOCUS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The local focus early Wednesday will be on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  minutes, due from both the Swedish Riksbank and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  left its benchmark interest rate unchanged on Sept. 4 as officials led by Gov. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mark Carney\f28 \uc2 \f2  await signs that Britons' earnings are to set to rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. economy's rapid growth over recent quarters has raised expectations that it could be the first among the world's big central banks to lift borrowing costs from the ultra-low levels that have prevailed since the 2008 financial crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sweden's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , meanwhile, is still very much in dovish mode. It left its benchmark rate at a record low 0.25% two weeks ago and the signs are that it may need to ease policy still further if inflation doesn't rise during this autumn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will announce its latest interest rate decision Wednesday but that is due after European markets close. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par  \par EUR/NOK \par Latest 0150 GMT 8.2938-94 \par Previous 2150 GMT 8.2925-3014 \par %Chg 0.00 \par  \par EUR/SEK \par Latest 0150 GMT 9.2264-340 \par Previous 2150 GMT 9.2227-308 \par %Chg +0.04 \par  \par USD/NOK \par Latest 0150 GMT 6.4036-72 \par Previous 2150 GMT 6.3992-4051 \par %Chg +0.05 \par  \par USD/SEK \par Latest 0150 GMT 7.1238-88 \par Previous 2150 GMT 7.1170-223 \par %Chg +0.09 \par  \par  \par NORDIC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Attention in the days ahead will be on the efforts of Swedish Prime Minister elect Stefan Lofven to form a new government.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "For now, both Swedish bonds and the SEK suffer from a small but palpable political risk premium," SEB analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, the Swedish krona will primarily take its lead from Riksbank policy with a rate cut from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the autumn looking a distinct possibility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nykredit markets said in a note that it expects the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to bow to pressure from low inflation and cut its benchmark rate to 0.1% this year. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCK INDEXES: \par  \par OMXN40  1355.95  -5.16 -0.4% \par OMXS30  1395.29  +0.2% +2.07 \par OBX      560.88  +0.82 +0.2% \par OMXC20   752.34  -6.71 -0.9% \par OMXH25  2919.06 -18.43 -0.6% \par  \par Brent    $98.91 -$0.14 -0.1% \par  \par NORDIC MARKETS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nordic markets closed mixed Tuesday as investors consider their options ahead of the conclusion of a U.S. Federal Reserve policy meeting which wraps up Wednesday. Leading the losses were some big Danish and Finnish firms including Vestas and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nokia\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The focus Wednesday will on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  statements in Sweden, the U.K. and the U.S., as well as a range of other macro releases including inflation data in the form of final EU HICP and U.S. CPI. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures were lower in Asian trade following gains in the U.S. session after the head of the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries said the oil producers' cartel could lower its production target for next year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders are waiting for news from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's two-day policy-making meeting, and the latest U.S. oil storage data from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Energy Information Administration\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Data from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 American Petroleum Institute\f28 \uc2 \f2  for the week ended Sept. 12 showed a 3.3-million-barrel increase in crude stocks, a 1.2-million-barrel drawdown from gasoline supplies and a 1-million-barrel build in distillate inventories, according to industry sources.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brent crude was 14 cents lower at $98.91/bbl, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  was 17 cents lower at $94.71/bbl. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEWS ROUNDUP: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: Denmark sold two-year debt with a negative yield at an auction Tuesday for the first time in more than two years, amid expectations that the country's recent rate cut will be followed by more.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINLAND: Finnish construction company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lemminkainen Oyj\f28 \uc2 \f2  (LEM1S.HE) said it has signed an agreement to renovate the Finnish Parliament building and carry out modifications at the Sibelius Academy building where the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Parliament\f28 \uc2 \f2  will be relocated for the duration of the renovation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINLAND: Finnish stainless steel company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Outokumpu Oyj\f28 \uc2 \f2  (OUT1V.HE) said it is considering the issuance of a new senior secured fixed rate bond with a maturity of five years and a target size of approximately EUR200 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINLAND: The \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can further expand its monetary support if low inflation persists too long, ECB Governing Council member \f28 \uc2 \f2 Erkki Liikanen\f28 \uc2 \f2  said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: Swedish retailer \f28 \uc2 \f2 ICA Gruppen AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  (ICA.SK) said it has signed a licensing agreement with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Starbucks Coffee Company\f28 \uc2 \f2  to open \f28 \uc2 \f2 Starbucks\f28 \uc2 \f2  retail stores in its Swedish stores.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NORWAY: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boston Properties Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (BXP) has agreed to sell a 45% interest in three buildings in New York City and Boston to affiliates of Norway's sovereign-wealth fund for $1.5 billion. The deal with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  Investment Management involves stakes in 601 Lexington Avenue in New York City, as well as the Atlantic Wharf Office Building and 100 Federal Street in Boston.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Write to Charles Duxbury at charles.duxbury@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 16, 2014 22:24 ET (02:24 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140915ea9f0004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
