\par\par \b \uc2 NORDIC MORNING BRIEFING: Markets Await Italy Election Results\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katarina Gustafsson   \par \uc2 4,002 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2013\par 04:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR-All times GMT \par  \par Macro \par n/a Italian parliamentary \par elections conclude O \par 0145 China \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  flash \par manufacturing PMI for Feb \par 1000 Italy bond auction \par  \par Nordic Equities \par n/a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Songa Offshore SE\f28 \uc2 \f2  4Q \par  \par Other Equities \par n/a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lowe's Cos\f28 \uc2 \f2 . 4Q \par  \par  \par IN FOCUS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The election in Italy is set to take centre stage for market participants Monday. Some attention is also expected to be directed towards Chinese manufacturing data and U.S. budget talks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Amid the eurozone turmoil, the Italian vote is one of the most globally watched for decades and the candidacy of the country's former Prime Minister \uc2 Silvio Berlusconi\uc2  has received a lot of attention ahead of the election. The resurgence of the controversial ex-premier, who resigned in November 2011, has made markets anxious as Italy battles with a grave debt crisis and investors are keen to see a leader who can push through restructuring efforts.\par \par \uc2 The elections, which end when polls close Monday, are being monitored by international investors and Italy's European partners. Results are expected from late Monday into Tuesday morning.\par \par \uc2 In a note published ahead of the election, SEB said that the election is pointing towards an instable mid-left-coalition with a wide range of political parties.\par \par \uc2 "The election results are expected to confirm that it is reasonable to have a continued large heavy political risk premium attached to the euro," said the bank.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the politics in Italy, U.S. budget talks are set to receive increased attention this week as the White House and Republicans resume their discussions.\par \par \uc2 Another highlight this week is U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  congressional testimony.\par \par \uc2 "In light of last week's revelations [in the minutes from January's FOMC meeting] Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2 's congressional testimony this week will be watched particularly closely for any signs that he is more concerned about the potential costs of further quantitative easing," Capital Economics said in a note.\par \par \uc2 This week's key macro events in the Nordics include minutes from the \uc2 Swedish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and GDP data from Sweden as well as Denmark.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par  \par EUR/NOK \par Latest 0250 GMT 7.4744-78 \par Previous 2150 GMT 7.4828-922 \par %Chg -0.15 \par  \par EUR/SEK \par Latest 0250 GMT 8.4571-610 \par Previous 2150 GMT 8.4686-795 \par %Chg -0.18 \par  \par  \par USD/NOK \par Latest 0250 GMT 5.6680-98 \par Previous 2150 GMT 5.6697-748 \par %Chg -0.06 \par  \par  \par USD/SEK \par Latest 0250 GMT 6.4133-52 \par Previous 2150 GMT 6.4197-248 \par %Chg -0.12 \par  \par  \par NORDIC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swedish krona has gained significant strength versus most other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the past few months, largely because of an increased appetite for risk in the market, IG market strategist Michael Canborn said in a note. Sweden's continued strong state finances and a large interest-rate differential compared with other countries where the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swedish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier February kept its key rate on hold at 1%, also contribute to the strength, added the strategist.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, the U.S. dollar has started to strengthen towards the Swedish krona during the past few days after worries that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  will end its asset purchases sooner than planned, Mr. Canborn noted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Friday, Sweden's Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fredrik Reinfeldt\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it was up to the markets to determine the value of the krona, adding that his government favors boosting the country's competitiveness as the best way to offset the effects of a strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Talking to reporters in Dublin, Mr. Reinfeldt cited Sweden's lowering of its corporation tax rate earlier this year, as one measure the government had taken to boost competitiveness and to "balance the fact that we now have a very strong Swedish krona."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCK INDEXES: \par  \par OMXN40 1,164.03  +12.43  +1.1% \par OMXS30 1,205.10  +16.15  +1.4% \par OB       434.62   +1.27  +0.3% \par OMXC20   539.49   +3.36  +0.6% \par OMXH25 2,369.87  +24.22  +1.0% \par  \par Brent  $113.81   -0.29   -0.3% \par  \par  \par NORDIC MARKETS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The underlying stock market sentiment is still strong, IG market strategist Michael Canborn said in a note. Looking ahead, a continued stock market rise is possible if the rotation from bonds to equities takes off for real, added the strategist.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key focus for investors today is expected to be on the Italian vote as polls close later today after opening Sunday. In macro data, Chinese manufacturing PMI for February will be one to watch.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants are also likely this week to pay attention to news from the mobile world congress in Barcelona. Nordic investors are set to keep an eye on Swedish network equipment vendor Ericsson (ERIC) and Finnish handset maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nokia Oyj\f28 \uc2 \f2  (NOK) which are among the string of companies participating there.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ENERGY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude-oil futures were slightly lower in Asian trading, despite news that China will raise domestic gasoline and diesel prices on Monday. Crude slipped between 3% and 3.5% last week, and money managers cut their net long position in Nymex crude oil futures and options by 6.1% in the week ended Feb. 19. In the same period, net long positions in ICE crude oil futures and options were increased by a modest 0.9%. Meanwhile, the preliminary HSBC China Manufacturing Purchasing Managers Index, a gauge of nationwide manufacturing activity, fell to 50.4 in February, compared with a final reading of 52.3 in January, \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC Holdings PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Monday. Nymex crude for April delivery fell 13 cents to $93.01/bbl; Brent crude for April delivery fell 29 cents at $113.81/bbl\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEWS ROUNDUP: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: \f28 \uc2 \f2 A.P. Moller-Maersk A/S\f28 \uc2 \f2  (MAERSK-B.KO), the Danish oil and shipping conglomerate, Friday posted a rise in quarterly net profit as higher freight rates and cost reduction helped lift the performance of the company's shipping business, which it expects to remain under pressure this year due to a glut of industry capacity and modest growth prospects.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: \f28 \uc2 \f2 DONG Energy A/S\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the Danish state-owned energy producer, said it is in dialogue with its principal shareholder, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , on possible initiatives aimed at strengthening its capital structure, including a potential injection of equity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NORWAY: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Renewable Energy Corporation ASA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (REC.OS), the Norwegian solar energy company, said it has received $27 million cash compensation from a silane gas customer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Atrium Ljungberg AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  (LJGR-B.SK), the Swedish property company, said it expects its net profit to drop in 2013, amid low growth rates in the Swedish economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINLAND: A Finnish food company said Friday it had asked authorities to green-light a plan to donate its products found to contain horsemeat to charities for the poor.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Food company Pouttu on Thursday withdrew more than five tonnes of kebab dishes from sale after the company's internal tests found traces of horse meat in products listing other meats on their labels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Write to Katarina Gustafsson at katarina.gustafsson@dowjones.com; Twitter: @DowJonesNordics [ 02-24-13 2209ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Katarina Gustafsson\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR-All times GMT \par  \par Nordic Macro \par 0830 Sweden trade balance \par for Jan \par 0900 Norway unemployment \par for Dec \par  \par Other Macro \par n/a US Fed Chairman Bernanke \par presents monetary policy \par report to \f28 \uc2 \f2 US House Financial \par Services Committee\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par n/a ECB President Draghi \par speaks \par 0700 Germany GfK consumer \par confidence for March \par 0930 UK GDP for 4Q \par 1000 Italy bond auction \par 1000 EU business climate \par indicator and economic \par sentiment indicator for Feb \par 1330 US durable goods \par orders for Jan \par 1500 pending home \par sales for Jan \par  \par Nordic Equities \par 0700 \f28 \uc2 \f2 DONG Energy A/S\f28 \uc2 \f2  4Q \par  \par Other Equities \par n/a \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.C. Penney Co. Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . 4Q \par n/a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Target Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . 4Q \par  \par  \par IN FOCUS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The inconclusive Italian vote has left markets in limbo and will likely continue to grab attention Wednesday while investors will also focus on U.S. budget talks. In the Nordics, eyes will be on Swedish and Norwegian macro data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The one thing that seems clear after the Italian election is that nothing is clear at all and uncertainty will linger on," Nordea Markets said in a note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The vote, which concluded Monday, was Italy's most globally watched election in decades and euro-zone debt worries flared up again this week as the election failed to produce a clear winner. Market participants had been keen to see a leadership with the potential to push through tough reforms to reduce the country's debt burden.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With no coalition getting a majority in both houses of parliament, ingovernability has won for now," Nordea Markets said, adding it sees a high probability of new elections within the next few months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Capital Economics said the political deadlock in Italy looks set to prompt a renewed bout of market pressure which may eventually force Italy to request a support package from the euro zone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition to Italy, investors will also be focused on the U.S., where Federal Reserve Chairman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ben Bernanke\f28 \uc2 \f2  is set to present a monetary policy report to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. House Financial Services Committee\f28 \uc2 \f2  after his address yesterday to the U.S. Senate Banking Committee.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Capital Economics said the testimony Tuesday confirmed that the Federal Open Market Committee is paying a lot more attention to the potential costs of continuing with its quantitative easing this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Bernanke still appears to believe that the benefits outweigh the costs, but the net benefits will continue to shrink as the Fed buys more assets this year," Capital Economics said, adding it is looking more likely the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  will slow the pace of its monthly buys soon even if it doesn't see the desired improvement in labor market conditions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key for market participants this week is also Friday's deadline for U.S. policymakers to agree on spending cuts. Unless lawmakers act to avoid it, some $85 billion in automatic spending cuts are due to be applied on March 1. The scheduled cuts were originally set to take effect at the start of this year before being pushed back for two months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the Nordic region Wednesday, investors can look forward to Swedish trade data and Norwegian unemployment figures in the morning.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par  \par EUR/NOK \par Latest 0250 GMT 7.4446-522 \par Previous 2150 GMT 7.4444-530 \par %Chg 0.00 \par  \par EUR/SEK \par Latest 0250 GMT 8.4360-426 \par Previous 2150 GMT 8.4325-98 \par %Chg +0.04 \par  \par USD/NOK \par Latest 0250 GMT 5.7064-114 \par Previous 2150 GMT 5.7000-57 \par %Chg +0.11 \par  \par USD/SEK \par Latest 0250 GMT  6.4663-704 \par Previous 2150 GMT 6.4566-609 \par %Chg +0.15 \par  \par  \par NORDIC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This is a busy week for Swedish macro data and releases. Tuesday, the minutes from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swedish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's latest rate meeting were in focus, today trade balance data are due and Thursday investors can look forward to retail sales. Friday brings both PMI and GDP data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEB said in a note that after its recent move lower, NOK/SEK is back in a 1.11-1.17 range. The bank expects the cross to remain in this range for 2013 and, lacking potential triggers, SEB said it would focus on playing this range ahead of the upcoming monetary policy rate decision in Norway on March 14.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank said relative monetary policy will be NOK/SEK neutral in the coming month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Norway\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , policymakers have again verbally \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to try and stop what SEB said seems like an inevitable appreciation pressure on the NOK, while in Sweden, the Riksbank is taking a more relaxed view on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength. SEB added that Tuesday's Riksbank minutes gave a reminder of this theme as the minutes showed no real stress as regards exchange rate developments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCK INDEXES: \par  \par OMXN40 1,148.42  -13.22  -1.1% \par OMXS30 1,180.90  -18.54  -1.6% \par OBX      435.02   -2.91  -0.7% \par OMXC20   544.78   +1.37  +0.3% \par OMXH25 2,314.59  -55.52  -2.3% \par  \par Brent   $112.82  +$0.11  +0.1% \par  \par  \par NORDIC MARKETS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nordic investors are set Wednesday to continue to digest the results from the Italian vote while also looking towards the U.S. budget deadline Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The election in Italy became the thriller and illustration of political risk in Europe that we would have wanted to avoid," SEB said in a note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Uncertainty has risen after the polls closed in Italy Monday afternoon as the election has resulted in a political deadlock. The VIX volatility index Monday surged by 36%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IG market strategist Michael Canborn noted that since 1990, the VIX has only risen by more than 36% during six trading sessions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude-oil futures are slightly higher in Asian trading, buoyed by statements from U.S. Fed Chairman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ben Bernanke\f28 \uc2 \f2  in favor of continuing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's bond-buying programs. Prices settled at fresh lows for the year Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices are following Asian shares higher, but uncertainty in the euro zone over Italy's election result continues to weigh. Focus will turn to U.S. oil stockpiles data due at 1530 GMT and news from talks between Iran and world powers over its nuclear program.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nymex crude for April delivery is 13 cents higher at $92.75/bbl and. Brent crude for April delivery is up 11 cents at $112.82/bbl.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEWS ROUNDUP: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pandora A/S\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PNDORA.KO), the Danish jewelry manufacturer, Tuesday said it has decided to change its dividend policy to increase the pay-out ratio and to launch a share buyback program of up to 700 million Danish kroner ($123.86 million), as it posted a higher fourth-quarter net profit than analysts had expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: Minutes from the latest Riksbank policy meeting clearly showed the stark division between the four-member majority on the rate-setting board and a more dovish two-member minority.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NORWAY: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Statoil ASA\f28 \uc2 \f2  (STO), the Norwegian oil and gas major, began an investigation into January's attack at the In Amenas gas plant in Algeria, in which five of its employees were killed, and said the team would report by Sept. 15.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NORWAY: Norway's government cashed in heavily on its oil and gas fields last year, snatching a chunk of the gas market from Russia despite weak demand in Europe, the company that manages the resources said. But state-owned \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petoro AS\f28 \uc2 \f2  also renewed its warning about maturing fields, calling for a massive investment in coming years to keep pace with the rapid decline in oil output.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jyske Bank A/S\f28 \uc2 \f2  (JYSK.KO), the Danish bank, said it expects economic trends to remain sluggish as it posted higher full-year earnings from a year earlier, helped by increased net interest income.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: \f28 \uc2 \f2 GN Store Nord A/S\f28 \uc2 \f2  (GN.KO), the Danish electronic equipment manufacturer, said a Cologne court has dismissed the company's claim over a compensation case that it brought against the \f28 \uc2 \f2 German Cartel Office\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: \f28 \uc2 \f2 IKEA\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the Swedish furniture giant, has expanded the withdrawal of the sale of its meatballs in Europe to also include Asia and the Caribbean, amid fears the products could contain traces of horse meat.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: Denmark, battling a stagnant economy and concerns about a productivity gap with other nations, is planning to slash its corporate taxes in phases over the next three years to better stack up against regional rates. The move, which follows similar tax cuts in the U.K., Sweden and Finland comes as pressure mounts on Prime Minister Helle Thorning-Schmidt. A ruling coalition led by Ms. Thorning-Schmidt's Social Democrats unveiled the planned cuts as part of a wider "Growth Plan DK" aimed at creating 150,000 jobs by 2020.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: Danish jewelry maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pandora A/S\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PNDZY, PNDORA.KO), looking to reinvent itself after a string of rocky years, will slow the pace of store openings but launch online sales in Europe this year in a move that could further diminish reliance on third-party sales channels and heighten its appeal for younger buyers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Write to Katarina Gustafsson at katarina.gustafsson@dowjones.com; Twitter: @DowJonesNordics [ 02-26-13 2236ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Katarina Gustafsson\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR-All times GMT \par  \par Nordic Macro \par n/a Northern Futures \par forum in Riga \par n/a Riksbank Deputy Governor \par Wickman-Parak speaks \par n/a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buys \par 0800 Denmark GPD for 4Q \par 0800 Denmark unemployment \par for Jan \par 0830 Sweden PPI for Jan \par 0830 Sweden retail sales \par for Jan \par 0900 Norway retail sales \par for Jan \par  \par Other Macro \par 0001 UK GfK consumer \par confidence for Feb \par 0745 France PPI for Jan \par 0900 Germany unemployment \par rate for Feb \par 1000 EU CPI for Jan \par 1300 Germany CPI for Feb \par 1330 US GDP for 4Q \par 1330 US jobless claims \par 1445 Chicago PMI for Feb \par  \par Nordic Equities \par n/a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Volvo AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  investor \par day in New York \par n/a \f28 \uc2 \f2 SeaDrill Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . 4Q \par  \par Other Equities \par n/a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Best Buy Co. Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . 4Q \par n/a \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gap Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . 4Q \par  \par  \par IN FOCUS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economic data from both the Nordic region and abroad will likely grab investor attention in the Nordics Thursday, with GDP figures from the U.S. one of the key highlights.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The data on how the U.S. economy performed during the fourth quarter come as market participants watch U.S. budget talks--$85 billion in automatic spending cuts--known as the sequester--are due to kick in on Friday unless policymakers reach an agreement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Attention this week has also been on U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Chairman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ben Bernanke\f28 \uc2 \f2  who has been closely watched during his semi-annual testimony, and his commitment to quantitative easing was welcomed by markets, easing speculation that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  would end its stimulus measures earlier than planned.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italy will also continue to be in the spotlight Thursday as market participants keep a close eye on developments after the Italian vote resulted in a political deadlock.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the Nordics, attention will also be on local data including Swedish retail sales and the Norwegian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's announcement on planned \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buys in the coming month for the Government Pension Fund Global, the country's vast sovereign wealth fund also known as the oil fund. Some attention will also be on the Northern Futures Forum in Riga.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par  \par EUR/NOK \par Latest 0250 GMT 7.4772-848 \par Previous 2150 GMT 7.4707-82 \par %Chg +0.09 \par  \par EUR/SEK \par Latest 0250 GMT 8.4430-508 \par Previous 2150 GMT 8.4437-501 \par %Chg +0.00 \par  \par USD/NOK \par Latest 0250 GMT 5.6891-942 \par Previous 2150 GMT 5.6875-925 \par %Chg +0.03 \par  \par USD/SEK \par Latest 0250 GMT 6.4239-90 \par Previous 2150 GMT 6.4283-323 \par %Chg -0.06 \par  \par  \par NORDIC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swedish krona still looks to be preferred to the Norwegian krone as the Norwegian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has indicated they will act to halt a further strengthening of the NOK while the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swedish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is comfortable with SEK at (almost) any level, DNB Markets said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DNB said it projects the Norwegian krone will be in demand near term, strengthening against most its peers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We expect the NOK to continue to strengthen for the next six months. From there on, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stimuli will gradually be withdrawn, removing the appreciating pressure on the NOK," the bank said. "But we expect the NOK to stay at strong levels despite seeing a correction weaker."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thursday eyes will be on Norwegian retail sales, which DNB expects to have picked up in January after a few rather weak months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank expects no change in the daily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  purchases from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the Swedish krona, DNB said abating growth in Sweden and improved market sentiment will eventually weaken the SEK long term, but it still expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at strong levels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The development in the export-oriented Swedish economy has taken a hit, and the possibility of another rate cut seems to be increasingly likely," the bank said, adding that it has revised its EUR/SEK forecast lower due to recent developments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCK INDEXES: \par  \par OMXN40 1,158.57  +10.15  +0.9% \par OMXS30 1,194.07  +13.17  +1.1% \par OBX      435.52   +0.50  +0.1% \par OMXC20   543.04   -1.74  -0.3% \par OMXH25 2,349.47  +34.88  +1.5% \par  \par Brent   $112.27  +$0.40  +0.3% \par  \par  \par NORDIC MARKETS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The political uncertainty in Italy and the U.S. is having the largest influence on Nordic equity markets these days and will lead to volatility, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ole Jensen\f28 \uc2 \f2 , head of Sales at Sydbank, said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Whether Italy gets a new government soon is important also for Denmark and the other northern European countries. The situation in southern Europe influences the whole of Europe," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also the budget cuts that might be triggered by the sequester in the U.S. hold a risk, while a solution to the budget issue could influence markets positively.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the light of these two events, the macro data that is due is of less importance, according to Mr. Jensen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "German unemployment will be closely watched of course on Thursday and on Friday the PMI data for Italy and Spain are interesting," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There are only a few corporate reports due in the next days and these are of less interest, Mr. Jensen said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude-oil futures rose in Asia trading, as U.S. crude inventories rose less than expected on encouraging signals from the euro zone. Brent crude climbed back above the $112/bbl-mark after falling to a five-week low on Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nuclear talks with Iran didn't see any major progress, but ended on an upbeat note, which could put pressure on Brent. The parties agreed to meet again in coming weeks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The WTI-Brent spread narrowed to $19.12/bbl. Nymex crude was up 40 cents at $93.16/bbl, and Brent was up 40 cents at $112.27/bbl.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEWS ROUNDUP: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NORWAY: Norwegian oil officials Wednesday boosted the Nordic nation's estimate of undiscovered oil and gas by about 2.5 billion barrels of oil equivalent, or 15%, most of it gas in an area formerly disputed with Russia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: Danish state-owned oil and gas group \f28 \uc2 \f2 DONG Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it will divest assets, cut costs and receive an equity injection of at least DKK6 billion-DKK8 billion to restore its financial health after making a DKK4.13 billion net loss in 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: \f28 \uc2 \f2 NKT Holding A/S\f28 \uc2 \f2  (NKT.KO), the Danish industrial company, posted a rise in full-year net profit from a year earlier but said it has scrapped targets for revenue, organic growth, operating margin and earnings per share, amid increasingly tough competition.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: \f28 \uc2 \f2 SSAB AB\f28 \uc2 \f2  (SSAB-A.SK), the Swedish steel maker, announced it has appointed Hakan Folin, who has held several senior positions in the company, as its chief financial officer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NORWAY: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Subsea 7 S.A\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (SUBC.OS), the Norwegian seabed-to-surface engineering contractor, has been awarded a $285 million contract from Talisman Sinopec Energy U.K. Ltd. for subsea construction services in support of its Montrose Area Redevelopment Project in the North Sea.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Per Aarsleff A/S\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PAAL-B.KO), the Danish infrastructure contractor, reiterated its full year 2012/2013 guidance as it posted an increase in first-quarter profit, helped by higher sales.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINLAND: \f28 \uc2 \f2 YIT Oyj\f28 \uc2 \f2  (YTY1V.HE), the Finnish construction services company, said it has sold apartments in the Helsinki region and other growth centers to Alandsbanken's housing fund for EUR43 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: Swedish furniture giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 IKEA\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it is removing wiener sausages from its stores in five countries after tests revealed "indications of horse DNA."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: Sweden will offer a total of 3.5 billion Swedish kronor ($543.9 million) of 1.50% bonds maturing 2023 at an auction March 6, the National Debt Office said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Write to Katarina Gustafsson at katarina.gustafsson@dowjones.com; Twitter: @DowJonesNordics\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Clemens Bomsdorf in Copenhagen contributed to this report) [ 02-27-13 2159ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 77791   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020130225e92p0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
