\par\par \b \uc2 NORDIC MORNING BRIEFING: Norway Oil Strike Ends; Now Data In Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sven Grundberg   \par \uc2 1,504 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juli 2012\par 04:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR - All times GMT \par Nordic Macro \par  \par 0600 Finland Industrial Production \par 0700 Denmark HICP Inflation \par 0730 Sweden Industrial Production \par 0800 Lithuania CPI Inflation \par 0800 Norway CPI-ATE Inflation \par  \par Other Macro \par  \par 0645 France Industrial Production \par 0800 Italy Industrial Production \par 0830 UK Industrial Production \par 1400 US Labor Turnover Survey \par NA   German court hears appeals against \par eurozone fiscal treaty \par NA   EU Finance Ministers meet in Brussels \par  \par Nordic Equities \par  \par 0600 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Axis Communications\f28 \uc2 \f2  2Q \par NA   Alliance Oil 2Q Op Update \par NA   Brinova 2Q \par NA   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oresund Investment\f28 \uc2 \f2  2Q \par  \par IN FOCUS: \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Norway\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to end an offshore workers' strike just minutes ahead of a threatened lockout at the country's oil production sites, the labour ministry said Tuesday. "The strike is over," ministry spokesman Jan Richard Kjelstrup said. Brent prices tumbled as a result.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Jan Hodneland, a negotiator for the Norwegian Oil Industry Association, or OLF, employers' group, said the government made a "responsible choice."\par \par \uc2 The Norwegian oil bosses had sought government action Monday in a pension dispute with hundreds of workers that culminated in the industry lockout threat in Western Europe's largest oil and gas producer.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday sees a slew of data releases in the Nordics including Swedish industrial production figures, as well as June inflation data from Norway and Denmark.\par \par \uc2 Apart from Nordic data releases, focus will remain on developments in the wider euro zone. A constitutional court in Germany is set to have a first hearing over the constitutionality of Europe's new bailout fund, the European Stability Mechanism, and fiscal pact.\par \par \uc2 Although the pacts were backed by both houses of the German parliament, they don't become law until German President Joachim Gauk has signed them. The president was asked by the German federal constitutional court in Karlsruhe to delay his signature so that the court would have time to prove the constitutionality of the pacts.\par \par \uc2 The ESM and European fiscal pact are considered key to fortifying the euro zone. However, political adversaries have filed legal challenges against the new laws before Germany's highest court. The ESM was expected to be launched later this month, pending ratification by national governments.\par \par \uc2 As to the specifics of the Nordic data releases, Danish HICP-inflation is seen at 2.2% on the year in June and negative 0.2% on the month, while Norway's CPI-ATE inflation is seen at 1.6% on the year and 0.1% on the month, according to economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Swedish industrial production in May is seen decreasing 4.8% compared to the same month a year ago, as demand for Swedish exports fades amid a weakening euro zone economy. However, the May figure is seen 0.6% higher compared to April.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par  \par EUR/NOK \par Latest 0150 GMT 7.4655-722 \par Previous 2150 GMT 7.4832-99 \par %Chg -0.24 \par  \par EUR/SEK \par Latest 0150 GMT 8.6177-256 \par Previous 2150 GMT 8.6222-98 \par %Chg  -0.05 \par  \par USD/NOK \par Latest 0150 GMT 6.0691-734 \par Previous 2150 GMT 6.0782-825 \par %Chg  -0.15 \par  \par USD/SEK \par Latest 0150 GMT 7.0057-108 \par Previous 2150 GMT 7.0033-82 \par %Chg  +0.04 \par  \par NORDIC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Monday, EUR/SEK was trading close to the 11-year low printed last Thursday, after the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announced a 25 basis points rate cut. The cut increased Sweden's rate differential, as the Riksbank one day earlier opted to stay its hand and leave its key rate at 1.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nordea strategists said Monday they were surprised by the extent and speed of the SEK strengthening. The bank revised its EUR/SEK forecasts downwards, and now sees the cross trading at 8.70 in early October.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bank noted a sharp contrast between the Swedish Riksbank's unwillingness to stimulate the economy, and other countries, competing against each other in stimulus packages and measures to weaken their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also, the Swedish economy so far remains resilient, while "prospects for euro-zone growth have continued to fade," the bank said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, later this fall, Nordea expects a softer Swedish economy and lower rate differential. That would weigh on the krona, and the bank forecasts EUR/SEK at 8.85 when the year ends.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As for the EUR/NOK, Nordea said trade is likely to be range-bound until \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  starts hiking rates, which it believes the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will do in 2013. Hence, Nordea forecasts EUR/NOK at 7.50 both in three months time, and at year-end.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCK INDEXES: \par  \par OMXN40  961.27   -3.51   -0.4% \par OMXS30 1020.44   -3.43   -0.3% \par OBX     372.88   -2.11   -0.6% \par OMXC20  458.03   -2.17   -0.5% \par OMXH25 1869.56  -31.15   -0.8% \par  \par Brent  $98.70  -$1.62   -1.6% \par  \par NORDIC MARKETS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nordea equity strategist Magnus Dagel said trading on Nordic markets looks to be calm and cautions ahead of the earnings season that kicks off in earnest as from next week, when many regional heavy weights are due to report second quarter financial.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Focus now is gradually shifting back from macro to companies," Mr. Dagel said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He added that investors are likely to keep a close eye on major U.S. companies that are due to report earnings this week, especially after earlier profit warnings from the likes of \f28 \uc2 \f2 PepsiCo Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PEP), \f28 \uc2 \f2 FedEx Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (FDX) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Procter & Gamble Co\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PG), who all cited concerns about developments in Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It does looks fairly mixed as far as results go, with profit estimates having been downgraded somewhat ahead of this earnings season," Mr. Dagel said. "But it still seems as if results will be on an alright level, maybe somewhat lower compared to the previous quarter," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bellwether \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alcoa\f28 \uc2 \f2  swung to a 2Q loss as aluminum prices continue to decline and as special items weigh on the aluminum giant's bottom-line result. Adjusted EPS came in at 6c on revenue of $5.96 billion. Analysts predicted profit of 5c a share and revenue of $5.81 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ENERGY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brent crude fell below $100 in Asia Tuesday, hours after a Norwegian crude supply disruption due to an oil workers' strike was ended by the government.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for delivery in August dived 77 cents to $85.22 a barrel and Brent North Sea crude for August delivery plunged $1.62 to $98.70.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A resumption of crude production in Norway after the government stepped in to end an offshore workers' strike was pulling prices south, said Nick Trevethan, senior commodities strategist for ANZ Research.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "After the government stepped in and ordered a settlement to the strike, that's returned one to two million barrels per day of oil to the market. That's having a relatively bearish impact on prices right now," he told AFP.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Authorities \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  just minutes ahead of a threatened lockout of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Norway\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's oil production sites, and ended a 16-day strike by hundreds of workers in Western Europe's largest oil and gas producer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The workers wanted employers to reconsider a decision not to grant special benefits to those who wish to retire at the age of 62, three years before the legal retirement age in the field and five years before the country-wide age.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The lockout, which was to have been enforced from midnight on Monday, had loomed after talks between employers and unions failed to end a prolonged strike which began on June 24.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Norwegian oil giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 Statoil\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the group most hit by the strike, said in a statement that it was "preparing to resume production at installations that have been affected".\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NEWS ROUNDUP: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: Maersk Oil, the oil and gas arm of Danish conglomerate \f28 \uc2 \f2 A.P. Moller-Maersk A/S\f28 \uc2 \f2  (MAERSK-B.KO), said the state-run oil and gas company Nordsofonden has joined the Danish Underground Consortium, which operates 16 fields with a daily production of about 180,000 barrels of oil and 14 million cubic metres of gas.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LATVIA's annual inflation rate continued slowing in June, to 1.9% from 2.2% in May, official data showed Monday. This brings the Baltic nation closer to its goal of adopting the euro in 18 months time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINLAND: Finnish start-up Jolla Ltd. is in talks with various hardware makers and other potential partners in hopes of unveiling within the next six months a smartphone that runs \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nokia Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s (NOK) largely abandoned MeeGo operating system, the company's chief executive said in an interview Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINLAND: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Finnair Oyj\f28 \uc2 \f2  (FIA1S.HE), the Finnish airline, said it carried 798,700 passengers in June, up 10% compared with the same month last year. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Finnair\f28 \uc2 \f2  traffic, measured in revenue passenger kilometers, rose by 9.3% as the overall capacity, measured in available seat kilometers, grew 1.3% compared with the same period last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Sven Grundberg at sven.grundberg@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Niclas Rolaner in Stockholm contributed to this report.) [ 10-07-12 0233GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 77791   \par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020120710e87a00003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
