\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Norges Bank Gov: Won't  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  To Control Krone Exchange Rate\b0\par\par\uc2 604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2012\par 17:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 29 May 2012 11:48 EDT *DJ  Norges Bank Gov: Greek Exit Could Send NOK Either Way\par \par \uc2 29 May 2012 11:49 EDT *DJ  Norges Bank Gov: Ready To Help Banks If Turmoil Increases\par \par \uc2 29 May 2012 11:49 EDT *DJ  Norges Bank Gov: We Must Live With Greece Uncertainty\par \par \uc2 29 May 2012 11:50 EDT DJ  \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  Governor: Won't \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  To Control Krone Exchange Rate\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Kjetil Malkenes Hovland \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OSLO (Dow Jones)--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Norway\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to avoid a stronger krone in the event of Greece leaving the euro and any resulting economic turmoil in the euro zone, its Governor Oystein Olsen told Dow Jones Newswires Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speaking on the sidelines of a parliamentary hearing about financial markets and asked whether \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  is ready to take action to avoid investors seeking refuge in the Norwegian krone, Olsen said the bank doesn't usually \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, and hinted it might not even be necessary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We did not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  [after the crisis] in 2008 to 2009," he said. "We have a steady policy on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . We don't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to affect or control the krone exchange rate. So my comments stop there. It's also uncertain which way the krone would move if the turmoil increases in Europe."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  has previously said the krone is not refuge of choice for investors if the financial markets turn bad.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Often, when there is real turmoil in the markets, the krone is not very [interesting], investors go to the Swiss franc, gold and dollar," said Deputy Governor Jan Qvigstad after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's previous rate meeting on May 10. "With less turmoil, Norway could be a safe haven, but when the situation is really bad" other options become more favorable, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Swedbank First Securities analyst Bjorn-Roger Wilhelmsen supported this view and said the krone tended to weaken when upheaval increased in the financial markets. "I believe that the trade-weighted krone exchange rate will weaken if Greece leaves the euro. I don't think much will happen against the euro, because the euro will weaken significantly. The NOK will first and foremost weaken against the dollar," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier Tuesday, Oystein Olsen told a parliamentary hearing on financial markets and regulation that the situation would be very challenging if Greece leaves the euro, and that "strong forces" in Greece and the euro countries are trying to avoid it.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Olsen also said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  is prepared to help Norwegian banks in the confusion that could arise following a Greek euro exit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think the uncertainty is huge, and we have to live with that," he said. "You shouldn't rule out that a Greek exit could lead to contagion in other countries, leading to more turmoil in the financial markets. If that happens, we will be on alert immediately, watching what occurs in the financial markets and the funding markets for Norwegian banks."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If needed, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  will provide liquidity as it did in 2008 to 2009, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Olsen also told Norwegian MPs that the idea of joint euro-zone bonds would be an "enormous reform," and would require a lot of work to design a system to replace each country's bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1411 GMT, EUR/NOK traded at 7.5247.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Kjetil Malkenes Hovland, Dow Jones Newswires: +47 902 27 908; kjetilmalkenes.hovland@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 May 29, 2012 11:50 ET (15:50 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120529e85t004nj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
