\par\par \b \uc2 Nordic crowns shine as refuge from euro crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Februar 2012\par 17:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Norwegian crown rises to 9-year high against euro\par \par \uc2 * Swedish crown also buoyed by sound finances\par \par \uc2 * Small bond markets limit chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , big gains\par \par \uc2 By Anirban Nag\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 21 (Reuters) - Investors seeking shelter from the euro zone debt crisis have driven the Norwegian and Swedish crowns higher as other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  once viewed as safe havens rapidly lose their appeal.\par \par \uc2 The strength of the two crowns, which are backed by top-notch credit ratings, solid public finances and current account surpluses, has so far drawn muted expressions of official discomfort unlike the Swiss and Japanese authorities' aggressive action to curb the franc and yen.\par \par \uc2 In fact, say analysts, the small size of the Scandinavian bond markets are a hurdle to further big gains in the Norwegian and Swedish crowns and indeed could see their strength drain away quickly, leaving little reason for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But for now the Norwegian crown trades near a nine-year high against the euro while the Swedish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands close to a 10-month peak struck in mid-January.\par \par \uc2 "For investors seeking safety from the debt crisis these countries are AAA-rated with low levels of indebtness and offer sound financial stability," said Carl Hammer, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, at SEB Merchant Banking, Stockholm.\par \par \uc2 "But because their debt markets are small, liquidity is an issue and many euro zone reserve managers do not look at them. So their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not gain to the full extent."\par \par \uc2 Demand for the two crowns picked up after the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  put a cap on the Swiss franc's exchange rate against the euro last September and discouraged safe-haven inflows that had pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to record highs.\par \par \uc2 Some of those flows turned to British government bonds, helping sterling, but after rating agency \uc2 Moody's\uc2  said last week it might cut the UK's triple-A ratings, Scandinavian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were left as the main beneficiaries in the near term.\par \par \uc2 Norway and Sweden are small, open economies that rely on exports. So stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hurt growth and could lead to deflation, which policymakers seek to avoid.\par \par \uc2 This was the threat Switzerland and Japan have fought in recent months by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which has caught speculators napping and left them nursing losses.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, the Norwegian and Swedish central banks have room to ease monetary policy to support growth rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say, and in any case there has been no sign of deflation.\par \par \uc2 Sweden's Riksbank is rarely seen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market while \b \highlight22\uc2 Norway\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2 , has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market since 1999.\par \par \uc2 "In our view we are unlikely to see more than verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  (from \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2 ) - and consider the probability of those low as well - which will only have a short-term effect on the Norwegian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Antje Praefcke.\par \par \uc2 SOLID FUNDAMENTALS\par \par \uc2 Sound public finances in Norway and Sweden have meant little need to borrow and issue bonds, limiting opportunities for investors. Figures from the OECD think-tank showed Norway's outstanding central government debt at the end of 2010 was $111.5 billion while Sweden's was $171.6 billion. Both were dwarfed by the $2.1 trillion outstanding for the UK.\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 Graphic on Norwegian, Swedish crowns vs euro\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.reuters.com/zac76s"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://link.reuters.com/zac76s}}}\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 Similarly, the Norwegian crown accounted for just 1.3 percent of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  turnover, while the Swedish crown made up around 2.2 percent. That compared with 85 percent for the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 This limited liquidity means swings in the two crowns can be fast and furious, with investors vulnerable to huge losses as they struggle to close their positions.\par \par \uc2 Between the Nokkie and Stokkie, as they are known in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, investors have shown a preference for the former partly because non-EU member Norway is a huge exporter of oil and because many are wary of Sweden's close trade and financial links to the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 Swedish exports account for around 50 percent of gross domestic product with the bulk heading to the euro zone. Norway, on the other hand, is a global oil and commodity exporter, so this week's rise in oil prices translates into more revenues.\par \par \uc2 "Right now the Norwegian crown has momentum on its side and if oil prices continue to rise, it could be headed higher," said Niels Christensen, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Nordea, Copenhagen.\par \par \uc2 "Its gains against the Swedish crown are also helping as investors like Norway's solid fundamentals more, but if global stocks get hit, then it will come under pressure."\par \par \uc2 (Graphic and editing by Nigel Stephenson)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /SCANDIES   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120224e82l001af\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
