\par\par \b \uc2  Norway Fin Min Calls Krone Overvalued;No Action Signaled \b0\par\par\uc2 425 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2003\par 21:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  STOCKHOLM -(Dow Jones)- Norwegian Finance Minister Per-Kristian Foss repeated Thursday that he considers the krone to be overvalued. \par \par \uc2  "The message from the Minister is that we think the krone is being overestimated," said Kaj Martin Georgsen, political advisor to Foss. \par \par \uc2  The finance minister's comments were made at a seminar Aalesund, a town in northwestern \b \highlight22\uc2 Norway\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Specific action like market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or interest rate cuts wasn't touched upon by Foss Thursday, according to Georgsen. He added that the Minister has mentioned previously that the krone is overvalued. \par \par \uc2  A Norwegian-language news wire, NTB, and a Norwegian  Internet news site, Nettavisen, reported the remarks by Foss. Some participants in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets misinterpreted the articles as the announcement of active measures to bring down the krone's strength. \par \par \uc2  The euro soared against the krone on the news, but when the finance minister's comments were put in the proper context, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surrendered part of its gain. Neither of the Norwegian-language articles directly quoted Foss as saying he intended to break the krone's strength. \par \par \uc2  The euro had jumped above NOK7.2600 from record-low levels around NOK7.2200 as rumors of Norwegian government action to weaken the krone circulated in markets, but fell back when Foss' remarks were clarified. Early afternoon in New York, the euro fetched NOK7.2414 in thin trading. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar Thursday, the krone reached its strongest point since April 1997, with the greenback sinking as far as NOK6.8505. In afternoon trading in New York, the dollar had recovered to NOK6.9050. \par \par \uc2  As investors worry about war with Iraq and higher oil prices, the krone has gained dramatically on both the euro and the dollar. With its vast deposits of offshore petroleum, Norway benefits when the price of oil rises. The country also boasts a hefty current account surplus and the highest interest rates in the western world, making the krone an attractive option for investors. \par \par \uc2  However, the strong krone hurts Norway's non-petroleum sector by making Norwegian manufactured exports more expensive in world markets. \par \par \uc2  Georgsen said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects Norway's general economic policies to help to bring down the exchange rate sometime during the spring. \par \par \uc2  "The strength of the Norwegian krone seems to be too high when we look at the real economy of this country," said Georgsen. \par \par \uc2  -By Rod Stone, Dow Jones Newswires; +46 8 545 13094; rod.stone@dowjones. \par \par \uc2  (Robert Flint in New York contributed to this article.) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030109dz190017u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
