\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Robust Norway crown unlikely to vex  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Natsuko Waki \par \uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 2002\par 13:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 9 (Reuters) - The Middle East conflict and higher oil prices sent the Norwegian crown to yet another record high against the euro on Tuesday but analysts doubted this would trigger any action from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The oil-backed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been almost constantly hitting record peaks versus the euro since January, reached comparable levels in early 1997, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling more than 20 billion crowns and slashing rates twice in two days. \par \par \uc2  But this time round, economists expect the bank to keep its rates unchanged at 6.5 percent when it meets on Wednesday. Moreover, they think direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is highly unlikely. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Norway\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is the third largest oil exporter after Saudi Arabia and Russia, has rates that are double those in the eurozone and which carry a juicy premium of 475 basis points over U.S. fed funds. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been surprisingly hawkish about trends in inflation and they are concerned about the tight domestic labour market, which gives them some justification for why they are reluctant to cut rates," said Juli Collins-Thompson, senior economist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  "We had such a rally in the crown and this is actually beneficial for their inflation profile." \par \par \uc2  The petrocurrency hit a record high of 7.6151 against the euro , according to Reuters data, and stayed close to its six-month peak of 8.6394 versus the greenback set last week. It also stands at a 15-month high in trade weighted terms, illustrating the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's broad-based appeal. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the current valuation of the crown is historically so strong. I'd be surprised to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepping in to reverse the trend given formidable flows," said Steven Pearson, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Halifax. \par \par \uc2  "The crown could go a lot higher in the short term given the strong Norwegian economy and prospects that interest rate differentials could widen in favour of the crown. Norway is a lot closer to raising rates than the U.S. or the eurozone." \par \par \uc2  EVENLY BALANCED \par \par \uc2  The situation in the Middle East is also benefiting \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that run large current account surpluses as any escalation in risk aversion highlights trade flows instead of capital flows as the main driver of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. \par \par \uc2  Estimated in 2001 by the IMF at 12.5 percent of gross domestic product, Norway's current account surplus is among the biggest in the world. \par \par \uc2  The domestic economy enjoys one of the highest growth rates among developed countries with high revenues from oil helping to insulate Norway from the global downturn. \par \par \uc2  Last month, the bank shifted to a neutral outlook for rates from an easing bias, reflecting diminished worries the downturn would brake inflation in Norway to below the target. \par \par \uc2  The bank shifted last year to target inflation of 2.5 percent in two years' time, dropping a goal of crown stability against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Economists predict Norway's annual inflation rate to have remained at a six-year low of 0.8 percent in March. Such predictions underpin market forecasts for unchanged rates on Wednesday. The consumer price index is due also on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The labour market is indeed tight with official data showing the unemployment rate at 3.0 percent in March, far below that of the United States or the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  "Norway has almost full employment now and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is concerned about wage growth and labour costs. But it's not in danger of missing its inflation target," Thompson said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also believe that ongoing 2002 wage settlements, where workers are demanding higher wages and pension reform, could buoy wage growth, but the crown strength and the revised 2002 budget to be finalised in June could cushion inflationary pressure. \par \par \uc2  "Downward pressure on inflation from the crown strength is likely to be offset by the tight labour market and the risk of bigger-than-expected fiscal expansion, so we have a relatively balanced picture here," said Karl Johan Haarberg, senior analyst at Nordea in Oslo. \par \par \uc2  "I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  learnt a lesson in 1997 that it can't fine-tune the market." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020409dy4900opz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
