\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CROSS - Mark/yen down, safe-haven Swissie up. \b0\par\par\uc2 814 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1998\par 13:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  Swissie gains on safe-haven flows. But mark hits nine-month highs vs dollar. \par \par \uc2  * Bundesbank leaves rates unchanged, as expected. \par \par \uc2  * EU's Santer says no G7 meeting planned on Russia, Moscow must sort out own problems. Santer says Russia crisis has limited impact on Europe, no need to revise EU autumn econ forecasts. \par \par \uc2  * Germany's Kohl sees growth up 2.9 pct in 1998-99. \par \par \uc2  * German July wholesale sales up real 2.7 pct on year. \par \par \uc2  * Japan MOF's Kuroda says yen correction has begun, Japan ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any time. MOF's Sakakibara says risky to have large \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions in current conditions. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Mark/yen\tab      at 78.42/47 against 78.68/70 in late trade on Wednesday and off 79.14 session high.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Markets watching results of Rubin, Greenspan meeting with Japan finmin Miyazawa on Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Swiss franc gains on safe-haven flows as Russian financial crisis continues. Mark/Swiss franc\tab      eases to 82.13/16 compared with 82.47/50 Tuesday.\par * Swedish crown slips back from 3-1/2-week highs as recovery stalls. Sweden's Riksbank says leaves rates unchanged after board meeting. Mark/Swedish crown\tab      trading at 4.5650/80 compared with 4.5385/15.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Norwegian crown slumps. Norway reports Q2 overall GDP rises 1.7 pct y/y. Statistics Norway revises 1998 GDP down to 3.0 pct from 3.2 pct. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Norway central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says did not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Aug 26-28.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Mark/Norwegian crown\tab      trading at 4.4800/50 compared with 4.4185/40.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  CROSSES REPORT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par LONDON, Sept 3 (Reuters) - A stronger mark battled with a firmer yen on Thursday and slid back against the Swiss franc after making gains earlier this week, analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The mark slipped to lows just above 78 yen in active trading amid verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials and on talk of Japanese repatriation of capital helped yen gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "All the talk seems to be about repatriation and the mark is a little undermined relative to the yen by indications the German economy is suffering from the Russian and Asian crises," said Rob Hayward, economic advisor at Bank of Boston in London.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers also said investors continued to unwind long dollar/yen positions to cover emerging market losses, buoying the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Reuters German Service Index blamed Asian financial woes on a fall in its main business activity index to 56.8 in August from a revised 57.3 in July.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The survey of business executives at 460 service sector companies, covering about 40 percent of the German economic output had some cooling impact both on demand and business expectations.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At 1100 GMT mark/yen was trading at 78.38/40 compared with 78.68/70 in late Europe on Wednesday, while the mark was trading just off a nine-month high of 1.7297 per dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official Haruhiko Kuroda said overnight the yen's correction from weakness has begun and that Japan was ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at any time.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par His warnings were echoed by finance ministry colleague, Eisuke Sakakibara, who said it was risky to have large \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  positions in the current conditions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The mark also slipped back against the Swiss franc as safe-haven flows and the continued unwinding of carry trade supported the franc, pushing it to a seven-month high against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The market is generally unsettled about where it should be looking for safe-havens," said Steve Barrow, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bear Stearns in London. "But there is a lot of uncertainty and people don't have a clear idea of whether Swissie should become a super-strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or not."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At 1030 GMT mark/Swiss franc had slipped to 82.15/18 compared with 82.4750 in late Europe on Tuesday and down from earlier session and one-week highs just under 82.60.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There was little impact from the Bundesbank's decision to hold its repo rate steady at 3.30 percent, as expected.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European politicians put pressure on Russia to continue the process of economic reform if it wants to be bailed out of its economic crisis.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The European Commission said it would back international aid to Russia only when reforms were assured and that financial aid would be "useless" without a Russian commitment to reform.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par German Chancellor Helmut Kohl said the European Union agreed that Russia's stability was critical for Germany and said the whole EU hoped the Duma would confirm Prime Minister-designate Viktor Chernomyrdin in its second vote on Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Elsewhere the Greek drachma fell against the mark to around 171.81/91 per mark from one-week highs around 169.35 it reached on Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, paul.casciato@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021217du9300q79\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
